# 🚀 Campus Mate — AWS Deployment Guide

This guide deploys the full microservices stack to a **single AWS EC2 instance** using Docker Compose — the simplest production-ready AWS setup.

---

## Architecture Overview

```
Internet
   │
   ▼
EC2 (t3.medium)
├── Nginx (port 80/443) ← your domain or EC2 public IP
│   ├── /         → React frontend (nginx container, port 80)
│   └── /api/*    → API Gateway (port 3000)
│
├── Docker Compose (all 8 containers)
│   ├── client (nginx, serves React build)
│   ├── api-gateway (port 3000)
│   ├── auth-service (3001)
│   ├── chat-service (3002) ← uses Gemini API
│   ├── data-service (3003)
│   ├── analytics-service (3004)
│   ├── memory-service (3005)
│   └── webhooks-service (3006)
│
├── External: Supabase (PostgreSQL)
├── External: Redis Cloud
└── External: Google Gemini API
```

---

## Step 1 — Pre-Deploy Fixes (Do This First)

### 1a. Fill in your `.env` file (root)

Open `c:\Users\Bhargav\Desktop\mini - Copy\.env` and fill these in:

```env
# Required — get from https://aistudio.google.com/app/apikey
GEMINI_API_KEY=AIza...your_actual_key_here

# Required — generate strong secrets:
# node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(64).toString('hex'))"
JWT_SECRET=<64-char-hex>
WEBHOOK_SECRET=<32-char-hex>
DATA_ENCRYPTION_KEY=<32-char-hex>

# Your EC2 public IP or domain (update after Step 3)
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://YOUR_EC2_IP,https://yourdomain.com
```

> **Note:** The chat service now supports Gemini. `LLM_PROVIDER=gemini` is already set in `.env`.

### 1b. Verify `server/.env` also has your Gemini key (for local dev only)

```env
GEMINI_API_KEY=AIza...your_actual_key_here
```

---

## Step 2 — AWS Account Setup

### 2a. Create an AWS Account
Go to [https://aws.amazon.com](https://aws.amazon.com) → Create account. Use your email, add a credit card (free tier available).

### 2b. Create an IAM User (don't use root!)
1. Go to **IAM** → **Users** → **Create user**
2. Name: `campus-mate-deploy`
3. Attach policy: `AmazonEC2FullAccess` (or `AdministratorAccess` for simplicity)
4. Create **Access Key** (Command Line Interface type)
5. Download the CSV — you'll need `Access Key ID` and `Secret Access Key`

### 2c. Install AWS CLI
Download from: [https://aws.amazon.com/cli/](https://aws.amazon.com/cli/)

Then configure:
```powershell
aws configure
# AWS Access Key ID: (from CSV)
# AWS Secret Access Key: (from CSV)
# Default region: ap-south-1   # Mumbai (closest to India)
# Default output format: json
```

---

## Step 3 — Launch an EC2 Instance

### 3a. Go to EC2 Console
- Region: **Asia Pacific (Mumbai)** `ap-south-1`
- Click **Launch Instance**

### 3b. Instance Settings

| Setting | Value |
|---------|-------|
| Name | `campus-mate-server` |
| AMI | **Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS** (free tier eligible) |
| Instance type | **t3.medium** (2 vCPU, 4 GB RAM — needed for 8 containers) |
| Key pair | Create new → name `campus-mate-key` → download `.pem` file |
| Storage | 20 GB gp3 |

### 3c. Security Group (Firewall Rules)
Create a new security group with these **inbound rules**:

| Type | Port | Source | Purpose |
|------|------|--------|---------|
| SSH | 22 | My IP | Remote access |
| HTTP | 80 | 0.0.0.0/0 | Web traffic |
| HTTPS | 443 | 0.0.0.0/0 | Secure web |
| Custom TCP | 3000 | 0.0.0.0/0 | API Gateway (remove after HTTPS) |

Click **Launch Instance**.

### 3d. Allocate Elastic IP (Fixed Public IP)
1. EC2 → **Elastic IPs** → **Allocate Elastic IP**
2. **Associate** it to your `campus-mate-server` instance
3. Note your **Elastic IP** — this is your server's permanent public IP

---

## Step 4 — Connect to EC2 and Install Docker

### 4a. SSH into your server
```powershell
# Move key to a safe location first
Move-Item "$env:USERPROFILE\Downloads\campus-mate-key.pem" "$env:USERPROFILE\.ssh\"

# Connect (replace YOUR_ELASTIC_IP)
ssh -i "$env:USERPROFILE\.ssh\campus-mate-key.pem" ubuntu@YOUR_ELASTIC_IP
```

### 4b. Install Docker + Docker Compose (run on EC2)
```bash
# Update packages
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y

# Install Docker
curl -fsSL https://get.docker.com | sudo sh

# Add ubuntu user to docker group (no sudo needed)
sudo usermod -aG docker ubuntu

# Logout and login again for group change
exit
```

SSH back in, then:
```bash
# Verify Docker works
docker --version
docker compose version
```

---

## Step 5 — Copy Your Project to EC2

### Option A: Git (Recommended)
```bash
# On EC2:
git clone https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/campus-mate.git
cd campus-mate
```

> ⚠️ Make sure `.env` is in `.gitignore` and was NEVER committed.

### Option B: SCP (Upload from Windows)
```powershell
# Run on your Windows machine — upload the project folder
scp -i "$env:USERPROFILE\.ssh\campus-mate-key.pem" -r "c:\Users\Bhargav\Desktop\mini - Copy" ubuntu@YOUR_ELASTIC_IP:~/campus-mate
```

---

## Step 6 — Create `.env` on EC2 (Secrets Go Here)

```bash
# On EC2, inside the project folder:
cd ~/campus-mate
nano .env
```

Paste this and fill in all values:
```env
NODE_ENV=production
LOG_LEVEL=warn

# Database (Supabase)
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.fuolmomfenpdtsryvihx:YOUR_DB_PASSWORD@aws-1-ap-northeast-1.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres?sslmode=require

# Redis
REDIS_URL=redis://default:YOUR_REDIS_PASSWORD@redis-15182.c10.us-east-1-3.ec2.cloud.redislabs.com:15182

# Secrets (generate these!)
JWT_SECRET=<64-char-random-hex>
WEBHOOK_SECRET=<32-char-random-hex>
DATA_ENCRYPTION_KEY=<32-char-random-hex>

# LLM — Gemini
LLM_PROVIDER=gemini
LLM_MODEL=gemini-1.5-flash
GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-1.5-flash
GEMINI_API_KEY=AIza...your_actual_key

# CORS — your EC2 IP or domain
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://YOUR_ELASTIC_IP,https://yourdomain.com
```

Save with `Ctrl+O`, exit with `Ctrl+X`.

**Generate secrets on EC2:**
```bash
node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(64).toString('hex'))"  # JWT_SECRET
node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"  # WEBHOOK_SECRET / DATA_ENCRYPTION_KEY
```

---

## Step 7 — Build and Start All Containers

```bash
cd ~/campus-mate

# Build all Docker images
docker compose --env-file .env build --no-cache

# Start all services in background
docker compose --env-file .env up -d

# Check all containers are running (should see 8 containers Up)
docker compose ps

# Tail logs to check for errors
docker compose logs -f --tail=50
```

**Expected output from `docker compose ps`:**
```
campus-mate-gateway     Up     0.0.0.0:3000->3000/tcp
campus-mate-auth        Up
campus-mate-chat        Up
campus-mate-data        Up
campus-mate-analytics   Up
campus-mate-memory      Up
campus-mate-webhooks    Up
campus-mate-client      Up     0.0.0.0:80->80/tcp
```

---

## Step 8 — Test It Works

```bash
# Health check — should return {"status":"OK","service":"API Gateway"}
curl http://localhost:3000/health

# From your browser, open:
# http://YOUR_ELASTIC_IP        → React frontend
# http://YOUR_ELASTIC_IP:3000   → API Gateway
```

---

## Step 9 — HTTPS with a Domain (Optional but Recommended)

### 9a. Point your domain to EC2
In your domain registrar's DNS settings, add:
```
Type: A
Name: @  (or www)
Value: YOUR_ELASTIC_IP
```

### 9b. Install Certbot on EC2
```bash
sudo apt install -y certbot
sudo certbot certonly --standalone -d yourdomain.com -d www.yourdomain.com
```

### 9c. Update nginx.conf to enable HTTPS
Add a server block for port 443 with SSL cert paths:
```nginx
server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name yourdomain.com;
    ssl_certificate /etc/letsencrypt/live/yourdomain.com/fullchain.pem;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/yourdomain.com/privkey.pem;
    # ... rest of your existing config
}
```

Then rebuild the client container:
```bash
docker compose build client
docker compose up -d client
```

---

## Step 10 — Keep It Running (Auto-Restart)

Docker Compose already has `restart: unless-stopped` on all services, so containers restart automatically after EC2 reboots.

To start everything after an EC2 restart:
```bash
cd ~/campus-mate && docker compose --env-file .env up -d
```

Make this automatic:
```bash
# Create a startup service
sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/campus-mate.service
```

Paste:
```ini
[Unit]
Description=Campus Mate Docker Compose
After=docker.service
Requires=docker.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
RemainAfterExit=yes
WorkingDirectory=/home/ubuntu/campus-mate
ExecStart=/usr/bin/docker compose --env-file .env up -d
ExecStop=/usr/bin/docker compose down
User=ubuntu

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
```

Enable it:
```bash
sudo systemctl enable campus-mate
sudo systemctl start campus-mate
```

